...
JDT_COMPLIANCE_PROPS.forEach(p -> props.put(p, complianceLevel.toString()));
```
Here `complianceLevel` was a `JavaVersion` field, converted properly via `JavaVersion.toVersion(...)` before being stored, so `.toString()` produced a normalized value like `"1.6"`.

Commit `7434506` ("Moved new JdtConvention actions to be local, where possible") refactored this to inline the logic directly into the `compilerComplianceLevel(String ...)` method as a lambda:
```java
public void compilerComplianceLevel(String compilerComplianceLevel) {
    ...
    extension.workspaceProp(JDT_CORE_PREFS, props -> {
        JDT_COMPLIANCE_PROPS.forEach(p -> props.put(p, compilerComplianceLevel.toString()));
        ...
    });
}
```
During this refactor, the `JavaVersion.toVersion(compilerComplianceLevel)` conversion was dropped. The lambda now closes over the raw `String` parameter (which shadows the old `JavaVersion` field name), so `compilerComplianceLevel.toString()` just returns the unmodified user-supplied string instead of a normalized `JavaVersion` string representation. This is a classic variable-capture bug introduced during refactoring — the conversion call was silently lost.

